<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maimonides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1138–<a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maimonides was a Jewish philosopher, legal scholar and physician born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> in the twelfth century. His family left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> conquest imposed pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians, and he eventually settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His works, including the Guide for the Perplexed, made him one of the most influential thinkers of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maimonides.jpg" alt="Maimonides" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Blaisio Ugolino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Moses_Maimonides_in_Thesaurus_antiquitatum_sacrarum.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> ben Maimon, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> by the acronym Rambam, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid dynasty</a>, at the end of the Golden Age of Jewish culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His father, Maimon ben Joseph, was a dayyan, or rabbinic judge, and taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>. At an early age he took an interest in contemporary science and philosophy, reading ancient Greek philosophy through Arabic translations.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1148 and abolished the protected status of non-Muslims in some territories. Jewish and Christian communities faced a choice between conversion, martyrdom and exile. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were forced to convert and had to wear identifying clothing, because the authorities suspected false conversions. Maimonides' family chose exile. For ten years he moved about southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> before settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and he wrote his commentary on the Mishnah in 1166-1168.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1171 Maimonides was appointed nagid, or head, of the Egyptian Jewish community; Shelomo Dov Goitein believes his leadership during the ransoming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> captives led to the appointment. He was replaced by Sar Shalom ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> in 1173, excommunicated him and quarrelled with him for years, and was appointed nagid again in 1195. After the family's funds were lost in a business venture, he took up medicine, which he had studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. He became court physician to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a> al-Fadil, the chief secretary to Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, then to Saladin himself, and he remained a physician to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> after Saladin's death.</p><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> on 12 December 1204. Medieval sources beginning with al-Qifti say he was buried near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, though there is no contemporary evidence that his body was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. A tomb in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> is a pilgrimage site and bears the inscription 'From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to Moses arose none like Moses.'</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mishneh-torah">Mishneh Torah</a> is a code of Jewish law in fourteen books that gathers the binding laws of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> and the positions of the Geonim. It is still regarded as one of the chief authoritative codifications of Jewish law and is closely studied in yeshivot. In his Mishnah commentary he set out thirteen principles of faith, which he saw as the required beliefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. His Guide for the Perplexed sought to reconcile Aristotelianism and science with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, and French rabbis banned it and burnt copies in Montpellier.</p><p>He also wrote on medicine, including a Guide to Good Health composed in Arabic for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who suffered from depression. Translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, it was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1477. His writings influenced the Scholastic philosophers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus">Albertus Magnus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duns-scotus">Duns Scotus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maimonides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…life more than formal statements of belief, though thinkers have defined principles of faith, most famously the thirteen principles of <b>Maimonides</b> in the 12th century, among them the existence and unity of God, the prophecy of Moses, the coming of the Messiah and the resurrection of the dead.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars such as Rashi, in 11th-century France, wrote commentaries on the Bible and Talmud still studied today, and <b>Maimonides</b> wrote his great code of Jewish law, the Mishneh Torah, in clear Hebrew in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Yigdal, Hebrew for &quot;be exalted&quot;, is a Jewish hymn that sets in verse the 13 principles of faith formulated by <b>Maimonides</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1180" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maimonides"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1180">What was here in 1180? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are like those who, though beholding frequent flashes of lightning, still find themselves in the thickest darkness of the night.&#8221;</p><footer>The Guide for the Perplexed, introduction &#183; tr. M. Friedländer, 1904 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73584/pg73584-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when I find the road narrow, and can see no other way of teaching a well established truth except by pleasing one intelligent man and displeasing ten thousand fools—I prefer to address myself to the one man…&#8221;</p><footer>The Guide for the Perplexed, introduction &#183; tr. M. Friedländer, 1904 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73584/pg73584-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…those only approach Him who obtain a knowledge of Him; while those who remain ignorant of Him recede from Him.&#8221;</p><footer>The Guide for the Perplexed, part 1 &#183; tr. M. Friedländer, 1904 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73584/pg73584-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when man is able to comprehend certain things, it does not follow that he must be able to comprehend everything.&#8221;</p><footer>The Guide for the Perplexed, part 1 &#183; tr. M. Friedländer, 1904 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73584/pg73584-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the plain meaning must be as valuable as silver, and the hidden meaning still more precious; so that the figurative meaning bears the same relation to the literal one as gold to silver.&#8221;</p><footer>The Guide for the Perplexed, introduction &#183; tr. M. Friedländer, 1904 &#183; on the figures of the prophets, after Proverbs' apples of gold in settings of silver &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/73584/pg73584-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
